<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perusine War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">41–40 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Perusine War was a Roman civil conflict of 41–40 BCE between Octavian and the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-antonius">Lucius Antonius</a>, backed by Fulvia, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. It arose from the confiscation of Italian land for veterans after Philippi. Octavian besieged Lucius in Perusia, the modern Perugia, through the winter until hunger forced a surrender; the city was burned and many of its leading citizens executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perusine-war.jpg" alt="Perusine War" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ColdEel / Cassius Ahenobarbus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mvt_troupes_Perouse_-41.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
